# `#[job]`

`#[job(...)]` marks one async `&self` method for scheduling. It is consumed by `#[jobs]`, which generates the descriptor and wrapper; it is not a standalone function attribute. Import it from `upwell::jobs`.

<SymbolMeta />
<SymbolSignature />

```rust
use upwell::jobs::{job, jobs};
use upwell::prelude::*;

#[component]
struct Reconciler;

#[jobs]
impl Reconciler {
    #[job(every = "30s")]
    async fn reconcile(&self) {}
}
```

## Syntax

Every job has exactly one schedule declaration: `every` for an interval or `cron` for a cron expression. Follow it with zero or more execution options.

```rust
#[job(every = "30s")]
#[job(cron = "@hourly", run_on_startup)]
#[job(
    cron = "0 0 * * * *",
    run_on_startup = true,
    timeout = "30s",
    jitter = "2s",
    max_runtime = "20m",
    overlap = CancelPrevious,
    timezone = Local,
)]
```

| Option | Accepted form | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Schedule | Exactly one `every = "duration"` or `cron = "expression"`. | Required. |
| Startup run | `run_on_startup` or `run_on_startup = true`. | `false`. |
| Timeout | `timeout = "duration"`. | No timeout. |
| Start jitter | `jitter = "duration"`. | No jitter. |
| Maximum runtime | `max_runtime = "duration"`. | No maximum runtime. |
| Overlap policy | `overlap = Variant`. | `Skip`. |
| Time zone | `tz = Variant` or `timezone = Variant`. | No explicit time zone. |

The macro checks the attribute's syntax and type constraints at compile time. It retains duration and cron strings, along with configured options, in the generated descriptor; the scheduler parses and validates schedules when it starts. `overlap`, `tz`, and `timezone` take current jobs API enum-variant identifiers, such as `CancelPrevious` and `Local`.

## Containment and method rules

Place the marker only on a method in an inherent `#[jobs] impl` for a component. The method must be `async` and take `&self`; a consuming receiver or `&mut self` is rejected because scheduled jobs run on the shared component instance.

The `#[jobs]` expansion resolves ordinary parameters from the root DI container for each invocation. Use their resolvable types directly rather than an RPC-style `Inject<T>` wrapper. `JobRunContext` is supplied by the scheduler and is the special per-run parameter.

```rust
use std::sync::Arc;
use upwell::jobs::{JobProgress, JobRunContext, job, jobs};

#[jobs]
impl Reconciler {
    #[job(every = "1m")]
    async fn reconcile(&self, store: Arc<Store>, cx: JobRunContext) -> Result<(), ReconcileError> {
        cx.progress(JobProgress::phase("reconciling")).await;
        store.reconcile().await
    }
}
```

## Return and generated behavior

A non-`Result` return, including `()`, is discarded and completes successfully when the future resolves. A `Result<T, E>` maps `Ok(_)` to a successful run and maps `Err(E)` to scheduler failure; `E` must be suitable for boxing as a thread-safe standard error.

`#[jobs]` uses this marker to generate a descriptor named `{Component}::{method}` and a per-run call wrapper. The wrapper resolves the component and dependencies afresh for each run, supplies the run context, calls the method, and normalizes its result to `JobOutcome`.

## Errors and runtime requirement

Unknown options, duplicate or missing schedule declarations, both `every` and `cron`, and invalid enum variants are compile errors. The scheduler parses and validates duration and cron schedules when it starts. The compiler also rejects use outside `#[jobs]`, non-async methods, invalid receivers, and unresolved ordinary parameters.

The macro only declares static jobs. Enable the facade jobs feature and install the scheduler/plugin to execute them; runtime schedule management, manual triggering, logging, and dynamic schedules are provided by the jobs runtime rather than this attribute.
